# Creating ShareDB Docs

The connection is open. Now for a document that several clients share.

The example is deliberately small, and it is the one ShareDB's own examples use: a button, and a count of how many times **anybody** has pressed it. The finished project is in your workspace as `sharedb-demo/02-docs/`, and it is `01-server/` plus one shared document; this reading walks through what was added.

## The Server Creates the Document

Two names identify every ShareDB document. The **collection** is like a folder or a database table, and the **id** is the particular document inside it. Ours lives in the collection `examples` under the id `counter`. One limitation worth knowing: collections are a **single level**. There is no nesting, no `examples/counters/daily`. A collection and an id, and that is the whole naming scheme.

Somebody also has to *create* the document, once, before any client subscribes to it. The first client to arrive would find nothing there, so our server does it on startup:

```{walkthrough}
title: What the server gained since stage 1
step: A document on the server
file: sharedb-demo/02-docs/server.js
region: doc
The requires at the top of the file have not changed. This block, between them and the server, is new.
@"backend.connect()": The server gives itself a connection to its own backend, the same kind of connection the browser has. The server is a participant here, not only a relay.
@"connection.get('examples', 'counter')": `get(collection, id)` in both places, on the server and on the client. It returns a document object; it does not talk to anybody yet.
@"doc.fetch": Asks for the document's current contents. It is asynchronous because the data may be in memory, as here, or in a database somewhere, and the callback's first argument is the usual optional error, which we throw.
@"doc.type === null": The test for "am I the first?". A document that has been fetched but has no type does not exist yet. (A document that exists has a type naming the OT type that governs it, the subject of the next reading.)
@"doc.create({ numClicks: 0 }": Creates it with a starting value, an ordinary JSON object.
@"startServer();": Note where this call sits: inside the create callback. The server does not accept clients until the document exists, so no client can subscribe to nothing.
@"'Document already exists'": The second run of the server takes this branch instead, and starts the server directly.

step: The server, wrapped in a function
file: sharedb-demo/02-docs/server.js
region: start-server
@"function startServer": Everything inside is stage 1 unchanged: the Express app, the static `dist`, the WebSocket server, `backend.listen`. It moved into a function only so that the fetch-and-create logic above can decide *when* to run it.
```

## Reading It on the Client

The client gets the same document by the same two names, and then does two things `get` alone does not: it subscribes, and it listens for operations.

```{walkthrough}
title: What the client gained since stage 1
step: The page
file: sharedb-demo/02-docs/src/index.html
The heading grew a live number, and there is a button now.
@"click-count": The span our client code will keep filled in with the shared count.
@"button id=": Anybody's press of this button will move every client's counter.

step: Element references
file: sharedb-demo/02-docs/src/client.js
region: refs
@"button#increment": The same `querySelector` calls as always.
@"'...loading...'": What the page shows for the moment between loading and the first data arriving. It might take a little time, and a blank span looks broken.

step: Subscribe, and listen for ops
file: sharedb-demo/02-docs/src/client.js
region: doc
Two hooks, two jobs: one for "the data is here", one for "the data changed".
@"connection.get('examples', 'counter')": `get` is local. It creates this client's reference to the document and sends nothing.
@"doc.subscribe": The part that talks to the server: it asks for the current contents *and* for every future change to be pushed to this client. Without it you have a document object that never updates.
@"updateCounter();": The subscribe callback runs once, when the subscription is established, which is why the initial render goes here.
@"doc.on('op'": Runs every time an operation is applied to the document, whoever made it, however many clients are connected. This is the live-update hook, and it is where rendering belongs.

step: Rendering
file: sharedb-demo/02-docs/src/client.js
region: render
@"doc.data.numClicks": `doc.data` is the document's contents as a plain JavaScript object, so reading it is ordinary property access.

step: Changing the document
file: sharedb-demo/02-docs/src/client.js
region: submit
@"doc.submitOp([": How you change a shared document. Note the array: a submission is a list of operations, even when there is one.
@"na: 1": The format from the OT reading: path `['numClicks']`, and `na: 1` to add one. The video points out that the json0 documentation lists many other actions, inserting into lists, deleting from lists, and more; the next two readings use them.
```

One thing this click handler does *not* do is touch the page. Writing `doc.data.numClicks = doc.data.numClicks + 1` would be the natural thing to try, and it would be wrong: nobody else would hear about it, because a change only travels if it is expressed as an operation. Submit the operation and let the `op` listener do the rendering, for this client and every other.

## A Miniature Shared Document

Here is that arrangement with a small stand-in for ShareDB, so you can watch two subscribers stay in step:

```{node autorun}
// A very small stand-in for a shared ShareDB document.
function makeSharedDoc(initialData) {
  const doc = { data: initialData, listeners: [] };

  doc.on = function (eventName, callback) {
    doc.listeners.push(callback);
  };

  doc.submitOp = function (operations) {
    for (const operation of operations) {
      const key = operation.p[0];

      if (operation.na !== undefined) {
        doc.data[key] = doc.data[key] + operation.na;
      }
    }

    // Everyone subscribed hears about it, including whoever sent it.
    for (const callback of doc.listeners) {
      callback();
    }
  };

  return doc;
}

const doc = makeSharedDoc({ numClicks: 0 });

// Two clients render the same document.
doc.on('op', () => {
  console.log('client 1 shows ' + doc.data.numClicks);
});

doc.on('op', () => {
  console.log('client 2 shows ' + doc.data.numClicks);
});

// Somebody presses the button twice.
doc.submitOp([{ p: ['numClicks'], na: 1 }]);
doc.submitOp([{ p: ['numClicks'], na: 1 }]);
```

Both clients see every change, and neither was told about the other. Try adding a third listener, or submitting an operation with `na: 10`, and run it again.

## Running It Yourself

For one browser, all of this to increment a number is absurd, and the video says so. The point only appears with more than one window. From `sharedb-demo/`:

    $ cd "02-docs"
    $ npx webpack
    $ node server.js
    Document created!
    Listening on http://localhost:8000

Open `http://localhost:8000` in **two or three** windows side by side and press the button in any of them: **every counter moves at once.** Nobody polls, nobody refreshes, and no code of ours sends messages to anybody. The windows all happen to be on your computer, but nothing in the code cares: users on different computers connected to the same server would be sharing the same document, which is where the last reading of this lesson ends up. Then:

1. Refresh a window: the count survives, because it lives in the server's document, not the page.
2. Stop the server with Ctrl+C and start it again: it logs `Document created!` once more, and the count is back to zero. The backend keeps documents in memory, so a restart wipes them; the `Document already exists` branch is there for backends where documents survive, which is what a database adapter would provide.

## Exercises

1. Complete the server-side setup so the document is created only if it is not there, and the server starts in either case.

    ```{javascript}
    const log = [];

    function startServer() {
      log.push('server started');
    }

    // A stand-in document that has never been created.
    const doc = { type: null };

    doc.fetch = function (callback) {
      callback();
    };

    doc.create = function (initialData, callback) {
      doc.type = 'json0';
      doc.data = initialData;
      log.push('document created');
      callback();
    };

    doc.fetch((error) => {
      if (error) {
        throw error;
      }

      // TODO: if the document does not exist yet, create it with
      // { numClicks: 0 } and start the server once it exists.
      // Otherwise, only start the server.
    });

    console.log(log, doc.data);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const log = [];

    function startServer() {
      log.push('server started');
    }

    // A stand-in document that has never been created.
    const doc = { type: null };

    doc.fetch = function (callback) {
      callback();
    };

    doc.create = function (initialData, callback) {
      doc.type = 'json0';
      doc.data = initialData;
      log.push('document created');
      callback();
    };

    doc.fetch((error) => {
      if (error) {
        throw error;
      }

      if (doc.type === null) {
        doc.create({ numClicks: 0 }, () => {
          startServer();
        });
      } else {
        startServer();
      }
    });

    console.log(log, doc.data);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(doc.data).should('equal', { numClicks: 0 }).run('Create the document with `{ numClicks: 0 }`', 'Created the document');
    wrap(log).should('equal', ['document created', 'server started']).run('The server should start inside the create callback, so it starts only once the document exists', 'Started the server after creating the document');
    wrap((function () { const other = { type: 'json0', data: { numClicks: 7 } }; let started = false; other.fetch = function (cb) { cb(); }; other.create = function () { started = 'created'; }; other.fetch(function () { if (other.type === null) { other.create(); } else { started = true; } }); return started; })()).should('equal', true).run('When the document already exists, do not create it again; check `doc.type === null` and take the other branch', 'Handled an existing document');
    ```

2. Complete the client side: render on subscribe, re-render on every operation, and submit an operation when the button is pressed.

    ```{javascript}
    const rendered = [];

    const doc = { data: { numClicks: 3 }, listeners: [], submitted: [] };

    doc.subscribe = function (callback) {
      callback();
    };

    doc.on = function (eventName, callback) {
      doc.listeners.push(callback);
    };

    doc.submitOp = function (operations) {
      doc.submitted.push(operations);

      for (const operation of operations) {
        doc.data[operation.p[0]] = doc.data[operation.p[0]] + operation.na;
      }

      for (const callback of doc.listeners) {
        callback();
      }
    };

    function updateCounter() {
      rendered.push(doc.data.numClicks);
    }

    // TODO: subscribe to the document and render once it is ready,
    // re-render whenever an operation arrives,
    // and add one to numClicks by submitting an operation.

    console.log(rendered, doc.submitted);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const rendered = [];

    const doc = { data: { numClicks: 3 }, listeners: [], submitted: [] };

    doc.subscribe = function (callback) {
      callback();
    };

    doc.on = function (eventName, callback) {
      doc.listeners.push(callback);
    };

    doc.submitOp = function (operations) {
      doc.submitted.push(operations);

      for (const operation of operations) {
        doc.data[operation.p[0]] = doc.data[operation.p[0]] + operation.na;
      }

      for (const callback of doc.listeners) {
        callback();
      }
    };

    function updateCounter() {
      rendered.push(doc.data.numClicks);
    }

    doc.subscribe((error) => {
      if (error) {
        throw error;
      }

      updateCounter();
    });

    doc.on('op', () => {
      updateCounter();
    });

    doc.submitOp([{ p: ['numClicks'], na: 1 }]);

    console.log(rendered, doc.submitted);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(rendered).should('equal', [3, 4]).run('Render once when the subscription is ready, showing `3`, and again after the operation, showing `4`', 'Rendered at both points');
    wrap(doc.submitted).should('equal', [[{ p: ['numClicks'], na: 1 }]]).run('Submit one operation: an array containing `{ p: [\'numClicks\'], na: 1 }`. Do not assign to `doc.data.numClicks` directly; a change only travels as an operation', 'Submitted the right operation');
    wrap(doc.listeners.length >= 1).should('equal', true).run('Listen for changes with `doc.on(\'op\', ...)` so later operations re-render', 'Listened for operations');
    wrap((function () { doc.submitOp([{ p: ['numClicks'], na: 2 }]); return rendered[rendered.length - 1]; })()).should('equal', 6).run('When another operation arrives later, the `op` listener should render the new value', 'Re-rendered on a later operation');
    ```

3. Collections and ids

    ```{mcq}
    Every ShareDB document is named twice over, as in `connection.get('examples', 'counter')`. What is the relationship between the collection and the document id?
    correct: Correct! The collection is a container, like a folder or a database table, and the id names one document inside it. Collections are a single level, so there is no nesting.
    wrong: You pass both to `connection.get`. Think about what each one narrows down.
    [x] A collection is like a folder or table; the document id names a particular document inside it
    [ ] The collection is a password and the document id is a username
    [ ] They are the same thing and can be used interchangeably
    [ ] The collection sets the programming language and the id names a variable
    ```

4. What subscribing adds

    ```{mcq}
    The client already called `connection.get('examples', 'counter')` and has a `doc` object. Why call `doc.subscribe()` as well?
    correct: Correct! `get` only creates a local reference and sends nothing. `subscribe` is what asks the server for the contents and for every future change to be pushed to this client.
    wrong: One of those two calls involves the server. Which one, and what does it ask for?
    [x] `get` is only a local reference; `subscribe` tells the server to send this client the data and its updates
    [ ] `subscribe` registers the license for ShareDB
    [ ] `subscribe` is only needed in order to delete a document
    [ ] Without it the browser blocks the WebSocket connection
    ```

